{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nMake the bridge execution path portable across local shells, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI,\nand bare environments without breaking checked-out workflows or environments that still use the\nlegacy reconcile vocabulary. The current production workflow contains about 185 lines of runner\nlogic, and `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` shells out to `gh workflow disable/enable` without a\nrepository argument. That best-effort GitHub-only schedule control silently disappears outside GHA.\n\nThe bridge/reconciler execution path is otherwise CI-neutral. The repository as a whole is not:\n`src/rebar/mirror_guard.py` intentionally reads `GITHUB_TOKEN`, hard-codes the GitHub mirror, and\ncalls `api.github.com`; `src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py` and `usage_report.py` optionally write\n`GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`. Those are GitHub-specific mirror-health/reporting features, not bridge\nexecution, and are documented out of scope rather than used as evidence of bridge portability.\n\nThe parent epic makes GitLab a required portability proof even though this repository is not\ncurrently hosted there. Ship a thin committed `.gitlab-ci.yml` as a contract artifact over the same\nrunner-neutral entrypoint. Vendor GitLab's own Draft-07 schema from\n`gitlab-org/gitlab/app/assets/javascripts/editor/schema/ci.json` at commit\n`776c20ad5675eecea0c2010433a94d98d745e921`; the immutable raw URL is\n`https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/gitlab/-/raw/776c20ad5675eecea0c2010433a94d98d745e921/app/assets/javascripts/editor/schema/ci.json`\nand its observed SHA-256 is `7568b544b4b95102f21e9fd89d769d54ee6f86b35d2fbb7f7b947d6fa9fc4dda`.\nThis is the GitLab-owned source documented by GitLab, not the distinct SchemaStore mirror. Validate\nit offline with the existing Draft-07-capable `jsonschema` dependency; record the source URL,\nrevision, retrieval date, dialect, and checksum in an adjacent provenance file. The GitLab artifact\nis exercised structurally and through its extracted shell body rather than by standing up a shared\nrunner.\n\n## Runner-neutral entrypoint\n\nCreate `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` as the one executable bridge wrapper. It accepts the\nexisting workflow `MODE` vocabulary and preserves its exact routing: `reconcile-check` remains the\ndistinct legacy `rebar reconcile --mode reconcile-check` diagnostic; `dry-run` maps to canonical\n`rebar bridge preview`; `bootstrap-strict`, `bootstrap-throttle`, and `live` map to canonical\n`rebar bridge sync --max-changes 10`, `--max-changes 100`, and unbounded `bridge sync`. The legacy\ndiagnostic exception is intentional compatibility, not a new old-vocabulary dependency. The runner\nowns `CANONICAL_EXIT_CODES`, required environment names, and a 60-minute/3600-second timeout, rejects\nshallow clones, and contains no GitHub/Jenkins/GitLab branch.\n\nThe runner also preserves the current post-reconcile contract in provider-neutral code. It captures\nstdout/stderr, validates the exact `BRIDGE_PAUSED: <json>` marker, exits benignly without ticket\ndelivery when paused, and after every successful non-paused pass invokes the existing strict store\ndelivery seam: `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always python -m rebar._store.push commit-and-push --tracker\n.tickets-tracker --strict` with the configured bridge-bot identity and provider-supplied\n`BRIDGE_RUN_ID`. The runner translates legacy benign 3/4/75 only at this wrapper boundary to\ncanonical 0, while operational 1 and invalid-invocation 2 remain 1 and 2; direct legacy callers\nstill observe 3/4/75. Provider wrappers map only the runner's canonical 0/1/2 result to job status.\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, and local documentation\ninvoke that exact file. Provider wrappers retain only provider-native checkout/tool bootstrap,\ncredential and signing-key materialization, timeout, masking/artifacts/notifications, scheduling,\nand exit mapping. The existing opt-in `RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` `gh workflow run` re-dispatch remains\nGHA-only wrapper residue unchanged; Jenkins and GitLab use their native schedulers and do not copy\nthat GitHub branch. Each provider timeout is derived from or contract-checked against the runner\nconstant rather than copied unchecked. Checked-out older workflows keep invoking legacy `rebar reconcile --mode`; the direct-engine route and its 3/4/75 results remain untouched.\n\n`scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the wrapper source of truth for the secret-name set and timeout.\nIts allowed exits are derived and cross-checked against\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py::_Disposition`, whose canonical column is 0/1/2.\nContract tests import the runner constants, parse all three provider wrappers, and compare their\nexit-code table, secret-name set, and timeout; the tests do not restate a second table.\n\n## Portable destructive-repair control\n\nReplace the `gh workflow disable/enable` helpers with the durable control already shipped by story\n`1104`: `refs/reconciler/gate` pause documents written through\n`rebar_reconciler._ref_lock.set_pause` and cleared through a new additive\n`read_pause_with_oid(...)` single-snapshot read plus the existing lower-level observed-OID\n`release(..., oid=observed_oid)` CAS seam, using the same configured remote and identity behavior as\n`rebar bridge pause/resume`. Both `fsck --repair` and the peer `doctor --repair` destructive path use\nthe same repair-owned pause context around their existing lock check and mutation. This preserves\nboth command surfaces while closing the provider-neutral schedule race; a scheduled pass on GitHub,\nJenkins, or GitLab reads the gate before mutation and exits benignly while paused.\n\nThe repair sequence is explicit and fail-closed:\n\n1. Resolve `who` through the existing `identity._git_email(repo_root)` seam used by `bridge pause`.\n   If unset, abort before any pause write or mutation with exact stderr `Error: <surface> repair\n   requires a configured git user.email`, where surface is `fsck` or `doctor`. Then read the durable\n   gate. If another actor already paused the bridge, abort the repair and do not clear that pause. Otherwise generate `repair:<surface>:<uuid4>` once per invocation, CAS-write it, and set the local\n   `pause_created` flag only when that invocation successfully creates the pause ref. Any pre-existing\n   pause, including one left by a crashed prior repair, is foreign: abort and leave it untouched. A\n   retry never automatically reclaims that pause; operator recovery/resume is required. Any transport, authentication, corruption, or\n   CAS uncertainty aborts the destructive repair.\n2. Run the existing provider-neutral `_reconciler_in_flight` check against\n   `refs/reconciler/lock`. HELD, corrupt, timeout, or indeterminate aborts the repair.\n3. Run the repair.\n4. In `finally`, clear only when `pause_created` is true. Add\n   `_ref_lock.read_pause_with_oid(...) -> tuple[dict[str, object], str] | None`: it calls `_ref_oid`\n   exactly once, decodes the pause blob using that same OID, and returns `(document, oid)`. Preserve\n   existing callers by making `read_pause(...)` delegate to it and discard only the OID. Verify the\n   returned document reason equals this repair's unique token, then call\n   `_ref_lock.release(..., oid=observed_oid)` so the reason check and deletion are bound to that exact\n   OID. A `None` result while `pause_created` is true means an operator removed the pause mid-repair;\n   treat it exactly as changed/replaced, surface a nonzero error, and never recreate it. A reason\n   mismatch or CAS miss likewise means the pause changed/replaced: do not retry against a fresh OID\n   and surface the condition. A foreign or changed pause is never cleared; a clear failure leaves the\n   bridge paused for operator recovery rather than silently reopening it. Do not use `clear_gate`\n   here because its internal ref reread would reopen the ownership-check TOCTOU window.\n\nThe lock remains the in-flight safety check; the durable gate is the schedule pause. This preserves\nthe currently-working GitHub safety behavior without a provider API and closes the Jenkins/GitLab\nrace.\n\n## Verification strategy\n\n- Run the real entrypoint in temporary repositories with vendor variables scrubbed and with\n  representative local, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab, and bare environments. Results and\n  repository/ref postconditions must be identical, and `gh` is absent from `PATH`.\n- Use a bare git remote to set the durable pause, attempt a canonical sync, and prove the pass exits\n  benignly without mutation. Exercise pause write failure, pre-existing foreign pause,\n  corrupt/indeterminate lock, ownership change before clear, and resume failure and prove destructive\n  repair fails closed without clearing another actor's pause. Drive both `fsck --repair` and\n  `doctor --repair` through this shared safety contract.\n- Create a real shallow clone (or drive `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` to `true`) and assert\n  the entrypoint exits 2 with exact stderr `Error: bridge runner requires full git history; shallow\n  repositories are unsafe (bug f193).` before any bridge operation runs.\n- Parse the production GHA, Jenkins, and GitLab wrappers and execute their extracted shell bodies.\n  The Jenkins `sh` body is a single-quoted Groovy string so secrets reach the shell/masking layer\n  without GString interpolation. Validate `.gitlab-ci.yml` offline with the existing `jsonschema`\n  dependency against the vendored official schema and verify its provenance metadata/checksum.\n- `shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1` is pinned exactly in the `dev` extra and committed lock. Existing\n  `uv sync --locked --extra dev` in CI and `make install` locally install its ShellCheck 0.11.0 binary;\n  `_build-and-test.yml` smoke-checks that exact binary version. The contract test hard-fails with a\n  setup error when the binary is missing or mismatched; it never skips. Shellcheck runs over\n  the script bodies extracted from all three configs, and a malformed wrapper/config fixture must\n  make extraction, schema validation, or lint fail.\n- Force the strict commit-and-push subprocess to exit nonzero after a successful non-paused pass;\n  assert the runner returns canonical exit 1, preserves the subprocess diagnostic, and does not\n  report convergence.\n- The production GHA workflow is still the scheduled artifact rebar runs, so the shared entrypoint is\n  exercised by the existing 20-minute schedule. No Jenkins or GitLab controller is stood up: both\n  committed configs use the same extracted-body, offline-schema, and contract checks.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the one runner-neutral bridge entrypoint.\n- [ ] The runner preserves the five current `MODE` inputs and exact route mapping: legacy\n      `reconcile-check`; canonical `bridge preview`; capped 10/100 and unbounded `bridge sync`.\n- [ ] GHA, Jenkins, and GitLab wrappers contain only provider-native checkout/tool bootstrap,\n      credentials/signing-key materialization, timeout, masking/artifacts/notifications, scheduling,\n      and canonical exit mapping.\n- [ ] A valid `BRIDGE_PAUSED` marker exits 0 and skips ticket delivery.\n- [ ] Every successful non-paused pass invokes strict commit-and-push; delivery failure exits nonzero.\n- [ ] GHA retains its existing opt-in `RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` re-dispatch and 20-minute schedule;\n      Jenkins and GitLab use native scheduling without a copied `gh` branch.\n- [ ] Checked-out legacy workflows and old-vocabulary environments retain the legacy reconcile\n      commands, defaults, messages, and 3/4/75 behavior.\n- [ ] `fsck --repair` uses the durable pause ref plus the fail-closed lock check and requires no `gh`\n      binary.\n- [ ] `doctor --repair` uses the same repair-owned pause context; pause/lock/resume uncertainty never\n      permits either destructive repair to proceed silently.\n- [ ] A real bare-remote test proves the durable pause blocks canonical sync identically from local,\n      GHA-shaped, Jenkins-shaped, and GitLab-shaped environments; an existing foreign pause is never\n      cleared, and only a repair-owned unique pause may be cleared by CAS.\n- [ ] A five-environment contract matrix runs the real entrypoint under scrubbed\n      local/GHA/Jenkins/GitLab/bare environments, with `gh` absent, and asserts identical\n      exit/output/ref effects and no vendor-variable reads.\n- [ ] A shallow-clone test drives the true shallow condition and asserts exit 2 and exact stderr\n      `Error: bridge runner requires full git history; shallow repositories are unsafe (bug f193).`\n      before reconciliation.\n- [ ] The shipped GHA artifact invokes the shared entrypoint and remains the workflow exercised on\n      the existing schedule; committed `Jenkinsfile` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` artifacts invoke the same\n      entrypoint.\n- [ ] All three provider configs are cross-checked against the runner constants and `_Disposition`\n      for one exit-code table, one secret-name set, and the 60-minute timeout, never against\n      duplicated unchecked tables.\n- [ ] `.gitlab-ci.yml` validates offline against GitLab's Draft-07\n      `app/assets/javascripts/editor/schema/ci.json` pinned at commit `776c20ad...`; its immutable\n      source URL, revision, retrieval date, dialect, and observed SHA-256 are committed and verified.\n- [ ] Jenkins shell bodies are single-quoted; pinned `shellcheck` runs over extracted script bodies\n      from all three configs in CI, fails when unavailable, and rejects malformed extracted\n      script/config fixtures.\n- [ ] GitHub-only mirror/report helpers are documented as out of bridge-execution scope.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py`: runner-neutral entrypoint and wrapper contract.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`: thin production GHA wrapper.\n- `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`: exact `shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1` dev dependency and lock.\n- `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`: smoke-check the locked ShellCheck 0.11.0 binary before\n  contract verification.\n- `Jenkinsfile`: thin committed Jenkins wrapper with safe credentials.\n- `.gitlab-ci.yml`: thin committed GitLab wrapper over the shared entrypoint.\n- `.github/schemas/gitlab-ci.schema.json` and `.github/schemas/gitlab-ci.schema.provenance.json`:\n  pinned official schema plus source URL, revision, retrieval date, and checksum for offline\n  validation.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py`: additive `read_pause_with_oid` single-snapshot\n  seam; existing `read_pause` shape remains compatible.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_advisory_lock_ref_backend.py`: prove the combined pause/OID\n  read decodes one observed object and preserves legacy `read_pause` behavior.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py`: durable repair-owned pause plus fail-closed lock safety\n  without `gh`.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py`: preserve `doctor --repair` while routing its destructive mutation\n  through the same durable repair-owned pause context.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py`: retarget all `run_repair` and string-based\n  `_reconciler_in_flight` test paths through repair-owned pause setup/clear; preserve pre-tag, no-event,\n  and refusal postconditions while adding doctor-side ownership/None/resume-failure coverage.\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_stale_channel_repair_heldout.py`: update string-based\n  monkeypatches of the removed `_reconciler_pause`/`_reconciler_resume` helpers and retain held-out\n  safety coverage.\n- `tests/unit/workflow/test_bridge_primary_workflow_heldout.py`,\n  `tests/scripts/test_reconcile_bridge_push_retry.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_reconcile_workflow_merge_driver.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_reconcile_workflow_no_shallow_rebase.py`,\n  `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py`,\n  `tests/unit/test_ci_actionlint_scope.py`, and\n  `tests/unit/test_ci_main_push_policy.py`: retarget the existing workflow parsing, push, merge-driver,\n  lint, actionlint, and main-push policy coverage to the thin wrappers without discarding continuity\n  assertions.\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_tickets_partial_clone_workflows.py`: preserve unchanged full-history,\n  blobless-checkout, and credential inputs in the thin GHA wrapper.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_run_pass_lock_lost_exit.py`: retarget its workflow-whitelist\n  wording/contract to the runner-owned 0/3/4/75 compatibility mapping.\n- `tests/unit/test_workflow_names.py`: reviewed and preserved as-is; the GHA artifact keeps its\n  filename and `Reconcile Bridge` display name.\n- `tests/unit/test_check_dco_identity.py`: reviewed and preserved as-is; its synthetic automation\n  exclusion fixture does not parse production workflow structure.\n- `tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py`: reviewed and preserved as-is; its historical bypass\n  fixture remains coverage while production delivery moves to the store push seam.\n- `tests/scripts/test_run_reconcile_bridge.py`: new runner contract including forced strict-delivery\n  failure -> canonical exit 1 and preserved diagnostic.\n- focused new workflow/runner/fsck tests: five-environment parity, three-config schema/contract/shell\n  validation, shallow rejection, and pause ownership/lock/resume fail-closed behavior.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` and `docs/release-notes.md`: local/GHA/Jenkins/GitLab usage, legacy continuity,\n  and GitHub-specific known gaps."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786300600003664001,"uuid":"cc3d1170-a25a-4590-98b5-4a5ecc1cc7ff"}